---
// What the app sends and keeps, in plain words. Each claim is the app's behaviour as written down in
// docs/decisions/0019-data-folder.md (the data folder), 0028-updates.md (the update check) and
// 0015-credential-profiles.md (Credential Manager). When the app changes one of them, this page changes with it.
import DraftNotice from "../components/DraftNotice.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT, REPOSITORY } from "../links";
---

<Base title="Privacy Policy · Kemable" description="What Kemable sends, what it keeps, and where.">
  <article class="prose-page">
    <DraftNotice />
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>Last updated 15 November 2026.</p>

    <p>
      Kemable is a database client that runs on your computer. It has no account, no server of its own and no telemetry. This page says what the app
      sends over the network, what it keeps on your computer, and what this website does.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app sends</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>One update check at launch.</strong> When Kemable opens, it asks GitHub for one file, <code>latest.json</code>, from Kemable's latest
        release, to learn whether a newer version exists. The request carries nothing about you or your data: no version, no Windows build, no
        identifier. GitHub sees what any download shows it, such as your IP address. You can turn the check off in Settings › Updates. If you choose
        Update, the installer is downloaded from the same GitHub release.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your connections, to where you point them.</strong> Kemable connects only to the servers you enter: a database, an SSH server, or a
        service's API such as Cloudflare D1 or Firebase. What you run there goes between your computer and that server, and nowhere else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nothing else.</strong> No usage statistics, no crash reports, no analytics, no advertising identifiers.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the app keeps on your computer</h2>
    <p>
      Everything Kemable remembers stays in its data folder on your computer: the <code>data</code> folder beside the app,
      or <code>%APPDATA%\kemable.app</code> when that folder cannot be written. Settings › Data shows where it is. It holds:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your saved connections, credential profiles and saved queries, without their passwords;</li>
      <li>the history of the statements you ran;</li>
      <li>your query pages, settings and the window's place.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Passwords</strong>, API tokens and SSH passphrases are kept in Windows Credential Manager, under your Windows account, never in a file.
      A Firebase key file stays where you keep it; Kemable keeps only its path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Uninstalling Kemable keeps the data folder unless you tick "Delete the application data". You can delete the folder yourself at any time, and
      remove the passwords in Windows Credential Manager (their names end in <code>.Kemable</code>).
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      This website is a set of static pages served by Cloudflare Pages. It sets no cookies and runs no analytics. It remembers your choice of light
      or dark theme in your browser's storage, which never leaves your browser. Cloudflare, as the host, processes the requests your browser makes,
      such as your IP address, to deliver the pages. The download links lead to GitHub.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>Kemable is a tool for working with databases. It collects nothing from anyone, children included.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If what the app sends or keeps changes, this page changes in the same release, and the date above with it. The history of this page is public
      in <a href={REPOSITORY}>Kemable's repository</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
